Optional is a container object —
it may contain a value, or it may be empty (no value present).

In other words:
Instead of returning null, a method can return an Optional<T>
to explicitly say “there might or might not be a value”

| Method                       | Description                                     |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `Optional.of(value)`         | Wraps a **non-null** value (throws NPE if null) |
| `Optional.ofNullable(value)` | Wraps a value that **may be null**              |
| `Optional.empty()`           | Creates an empty Optional                       |

Accessing the value inside an Optional
| Method                  | Use                                                     | Example                                |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `.isPresent()`          | Check if value exists                                   | `if(opt.isPresent()) ...`              |
| `.get()`                | Get value (unsafe — may throw `NoSuchElementException`) | `opt.get()`                            |
| `.orElse(defaultValue)` | Return value or default if empty                        | `opt.orElse("Guest")`                  |
| `.orElseGet(Supplier)`  | Return value or use supplier to compute default         | `opt.orElseGet(() -> "GeneratedName")` |
| `.ifPresent(Consumer)`  | Execute action only if value exists                     | `opt.ifPresent(System.out::println)`   |
| `.map(Function)`        | Transform the value if present                          | `opt.map(String::toUpperCase)`         |
| `.filter(Predicate)`    | Keep value only if condition matches                    | `opt.filter(s -> s.startsWith("S"))`   |
